{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for deployment note exact clean head 6f292a1eb02c3efa41039d768dbcb8fdcdc26824, requested by git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f80e926986b1d2d4912f42f56432c78d7d6e3291 and described by artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a286c4910b9c579966ba1d736f2132fc0287ed43. make vet test and diff-check pass; exact head and cleanliness hold.\n\nF1 BLOCKER — the claimed object graph is false as written. A tree cannot keep commit objects reachable. I built a bare repository, put a candidate commit in the claim tree as a 160000 commit entry, referenced only the claim commit, and ran rev-list plus fsck --unreachable: rev-list traversed the claim and its tree but not the gitlink target, and fsck reported the candidate commit unreachable. A tree can embed IDs or bytes as blobs, but that does not retain the named commits or cause push to transfer them. Specify an actual reachability graph, for example claim-commit parent topology that reaches prebuilt E and every still-needed candidate, and executable proof across receive-pack plus pruning. Until then an atomic inbox deletion can strand exactly the objects recovery needs.\n\nF2 BLOCKER — exact E is still contradicted by both recovery descriptions. The new table says P/P/live means “re-drain; idempotency makes it exact”; case 6 still says the head is computable from recorded parent plus candidates and repeats only three old cases. The note itself established that re-signing later changes timestamps and yields a different head. P/P/live must publish the already-prebuilt, recorded E without reconstructing it. Case 6 must delete the obsolete paragraph and cover E/E/live. Case 8 must assert recovery publishes the identical recorded E, not merely one admission, or it can pass while recomputing E-prime and never exercising the new invariant.\n\nF3 BLOCKER — the no-claim rule loses accepted work in the most important claim-loss state. Destroy the live claim at P/P before publication: inbox refs are already deleted, published equals mirror, and the no-claim rule classifies the workroom as complete/quiescent. The batch disappears without a log event, refusal note, inbox, or claim. Case 6 destroys a claim only while refs diverge, so it cannot detect this failure. A ruleset limiting deletion does not make loss impossible because the drain principal must delete claims and an implementation bug or privileged failure remains in scope of the deliberately destroyed-claim test. Cover claim loss at every live row, especially P/P, and provide a retained batch record that survives claim-ref loss or narrow the terminal-record guarantee honestly.\n\nF4 BLOCKER — multiple claims are not structurally excluded. One claim per run at claimed/\u003cgenesis\u003e/\u003cbatch-digest\u003e still lets two runs that both observed no claim atomically create two different batch refs when their inbox sets are disjoint, for example when a new inbox arrives between their listings. “Refuse while any claim is live” is a check-then-act, not a CAS. The concurrency group is an operational serialization layer, but the protocol explicitly claims structural exclusion and concurrency safety. Use one shared per-genesis claim/lock ref with an absent-value lease, or another atomic singleton. Case 10 must race distinct batch digests/disjoint candidates; an already-occupied identical claim ref cannot expose this.\n\nF5 MUST FIX — the start invariant is observational across two repositories and claim acquisition does not bind the published ref it prebuilt from. A move after reading P but before the atomic claim produces a stale claim and an anomaly rather than corruption, so the catch-all is conservative, but the text calls this an invariant without specifying the checks. Bind the submission repository to P during claim acquisition with an explicit lease/no-op ref update if the receiver enforces it, then re-read both repositories after the claim and classify any mirror race as anomaly. The mirror cannot be atomically included across repositories; say so.\n\nThe current GitHub ruleset mechanism is plausible: current official rules allow restrict creation/deletion for matching branches with a drain app in the bypass list. It remains correctly marked unverified before offering tier 1. Case 7 should explicitly attempt claim deletion, rather than leave “push any ref outside inbox” to implication.\n\nAll-case cannot-fail audit: cases 1–5 and 9 directly exercise their named subject; cases 6, 8, and 10 have the blind spots above; case 7 needs the explicit deletion attempt. No claim that was true before this commit was found newly false, but the new tree-reachability and structural-single-claim claims are themselves false. The four findings from git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8393d8d1949dc0bdbfc8d73f84f6b5a05509e2ba therefore remain open in substance.","body":{"finding_count":"5","head":"6f292a1eb02c3efa41039d768dbcb8fdcdc26824","probe":"/tmp/gitseq-claim-tree","verdict":"changes-requested"}}